{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My sabbaths you shall keep and My sanctuary you shall revere.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Torat Kohanim: 'Scripture warned concerning all the commandments.' And the Ramban, z\"l, wrote that the meaning of this midrash is that Scripture came to warn the servant sold to a gentile — concerning idolatry and the sabbath — and also the reverence of the sanctuary, that one should not enter there with shoes on (ברגלים) and should revere it; and the same reasoning applies to all the commandments, but it mentioned these because they are archetypes (אבות) to teach concerning all of them. And there are those whose text reads 'the one who warns here,' and if the reading is thus, they hinted that all the commandments are included in sabbath and sanctuary, and the discerning will understand — end of his words. And the explanation of his words is that the sabbath and the sanctuary are 'remembered' (זכור) and 'kept' (שמור), and it is known that all the commandments, both positive commandments and negative commandments, are included in them, and this is clear." "<b>I am YHWH.</b> Who gives reward for their fulfillment or punishes the one who transgresses them. And every place where it says אני ה׳, it comes to intensify the commandment — for the greatness of the reward or the greatness of the punishment. Therefore this section concluded with אני ה׳ for these two commandments. For behold a king who commands his servant and says to him by decree, 'Do thus and so,' and afterward adds to that commandment and says, 'I am the king who commands' — behold, this utterance comes to strengthen his command, and whoever rebels against it is liable with his life, and whoever fulfills it receives great reward. And so they said in Midrash Tanchuma, in the section of Bereshit: 'Go out and learn the giving of the reward for commandments. When Pharaoh said to Joseph, אני פרעה — how much he magnified him and gave him reward, as it is said (Genesis 41:42): ״ויסר פרעה את טבעתו״ etc. The Holy One, blessed be He, who said concerning every single commandment, אני ה׳ — for one, how much the more so! To teach you that there is no limit to the giving of their reward' — end of quote. The meaning of this midrash is that in every single commandment in which He mentioned אני ה׳, there is enormous reward and great abundance (עקב רב), and all the more so by an argument from the lighter to the heavier (קל וחומר) from flesh and blood. Therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, warned man to keep every commandment in which the Holy One, blessed be He, uses the language of אני — which is the utterance of the King — as he said (Ecclesiastes 8:2): ״אני פי מלך שמור ועל דברת שבועת אלהים״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "Reading ברגלים literally as 'with shoes'; the idiom of not entering the Temple precincts shod is well-attested in rabbinic sources. The variant reading 'הזהיר כאן' vs. 'המזהיר כאן' is reported as a difference between textual witnesses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:39.649999288Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}